{# ===========================================================================
   Component: one room card on /menu/

   Expects `loc` and `sdRoomIds`. Ported from the .loc card in
   _reference/menu-concepts/tajima-menu_mockup-1_location-first.html: name,
   area, blurb, a tag list, and a dish count in the top right, filling with ink
   on hover.

   TWO LINKS NOW, NOT ONE, added 2026-09-19. It was a single <a> around the
   whole card, correctly pointing at the room, but with the only "this is
   clickable" signal being an ink-fill that only shows up on hover, invisible
   at rest and to anyone who has not moved a mouse over it. Checked before
   changing anything: the link was real, just not visibly one. `.mchoose-card`
   is the container now; `.mchoose-card__link` keeps the original whole-block
   behavior (count, name, area, blurb, tags, same href as before);
   `.mchoose-card__cta` is new, an explicit "See the full [room] menu" link,
   underlined at rest so it reads as a link before anyone hovers anything. An
   anchor cannot nest inside another anchor, which is the reason this is two
   siblings rather than one link with a second link inside it, same shape as
   the mobile Locations panel's Directions fix.

   THE CTA POINTS AT #menu, NOT JUST THE ROOM URL: location-menu.njk's own
   header comment calls id="menu" load-bearing for exactly this, "a 'see the
   menu' link on a room page that points there", already used by the NAP
   plate and every full location template. Reused here rather than
   reinvented, so this lands a reader on the actual menu section instead of
   the top of the page.

   EVERY VALUE IS DATA. `name`, `neighborhood` and `summary` come from
   locations.json and are confirmed. The count and the tags come from the
   `roomMenuFacts` filter, which computes them from menu.json; see the note on
   that filter for why none of it is authored.
   =========================================================================== #}
{#- From coreMenu, which counts the rows the room's own page renders. It was
    a filter over menu.json, which answered a question no page asks now: a card
    advertising "34 dishes" against a page listing 39 is exactly the drift this
    pass exists to end. -#}
{%- set facts = coreMenu.cards[loc.id] %}
<article class="mchoose-card">
  <a class="mchoose-card__link" href="{{ loc.url }}">
    <span class="mchoose-card__count">{{ facts.served }} dishes</span>
    <span class="mchoose-card__name">{{ loc.name }}</span>
    <span class="mchoose-card__area">{{ loc.neighborhood }}</span>
    <span class="mchoose-card__blurb">{{ loc.summary }}</span>
    {#- The "N only here" tag (facts.exclusive, a bare dish count) is gone on
        request: computed and accurate, but a number with no noun read as
        confusing rather than informative. rareSections stays; those are
        section names, not counts, and read as intended. -#}
    {%- if facts.rareSections | length %}
    <span class="mchoose-card__tags">
      {%- for label in facts.rareSections %}<span class="mchoose-card__tag">{{ label }}</span>{% endfor %}
    </span>
    {%- endif %}
  </a>
  <a class="mchoose-card__cta" href="{{ loc.url }}#menu">
    See the full {{ loc.name }} menu
    <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M1 7H13M13 7L7.5 1.5M13 7L7.5 12.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
  </a>
</article>
